0,0,0,0,0,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The rule was not at fault. The program crashed because the module `collections` was never imported, so `collections.Counter` raised a NameError before any rule logic could be checked. I have added `import collections` inside the function. The rest of the program is unchanged; I have not re-run it against the train pairs yet.\n\n**Rule recap**\n- **Walls:** every same-colour piece of 2 or more cells is an outline (a wall).\n- **Dots:** every other coloured cell is a lone dot, and all dots are erased.\n- **Interiors:** a region is enclosed if it cannot reach the grid border through non-wall cells.\n- **Filling:** each enclosed region is painted in the colour that appears most often among the dots originally inside it.\n\n**How this explains the train pairs**\n- **Pair 1:**\n  - The red interior has dots 3, 3, 8, so it fills with 3.\n  - The yellow interior has dots 8, 3, 8, 8, so it fills with 8.\n  - The magenta shape is open, so its dot is just erased.\n- **Pair 2:**\n  - The red interior has dots 5, 8, 8, 8, so it fills with 8.\n  - The blue interior has dots 8, 5, 5, 5, so it fills with 5.\n  - The green shape has a gap, so its dot is erased and nothing is filled.\n- **Pair 3:** the dots are 2, 8, 8, 6, 2, 6, 6. Colour 6 has 3 votes against 2 for each of the others, so the interior fills with 6.\n\n**What the rule predicts for the test**\n- The red, green and azure outlines are closed, so their interiors are filled.\n- The orange outline has an opening on its lower right, so it is not filled and its dots are only erased.\n- In the azure interior, colour 4 has 3 votes, which beats colour 3, so that interior fills with 4.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    import collections\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    wall = np.zeros((H, W), bool)\n    for c in np.unique(g):\n        if c == bg:\n            continue\n        for comp in components(g == c):\n            if len(comp) >= 2:\n                for y, x in comp:\n                    wall[y, x] = True\n    out = g.copy()\n    out[(~wall) & (g != bg)] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase all single-cell dots, keeping only the outline shapes.\")\n    for comp in components(~wall):\n        if any(y == 0 or x == 0 or y == H - 1 or x == W - 1 for y, x in comp):\n            continue\n        cnt = collections.Counter(int(g[y, x]) for y, x in comp if g[y, x] != bg)\n        if not cnt:\n            continue\n        col = cnt.most_common(1)[0][0]\n        for y, x in comp:\n            out[y, x] = col\n        emit(out, f\"Fill enclosed interior with majority dot colour {col}.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Interiors:** a region is enclosed if it cannot reach the grid border through non-wall cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Filling:** each enclosed region is painted in the colour that appears most often among the dots originally inside it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The red interior has dots 3, 3, 8, so it fills with 3."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0bf17ddbf2a412892f97d20717eb1b9e9f810c0db488d734038ffa8c3fa3decb","task_sha256":"85e638b2d4ed9a4accb8bdfa03283a05832fdd43cdac772667a5c2ff8f625c6e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
